 ### Metric-Phase Fields (MPF)
-- **Definition:** MPFs decouple metric proximity from phase/sign information by learning two continuous fields: an unsigned metric field $r(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0$ and a phase field $\theta(\mathbf{x})$ [S3, unofficial]. The phase field induces a bounded indicator $P(\mathbf{x}) = \tanh(\beta \theta(\mathbf{x})) \in (-1, 1)$, where $\beta > 0$ is a learnable scaling coefficient controlling the sharpness of the phase transition [S3, unofficial]. The composite field is $\phi(\mathbf{x}) = r(\mathbf{x})P(\mathbf{x}) + \theta(\mathbf{x})$ [S3, unofficial]. The surface is the zero level set $\{\mathbf{x} : \phi(\mathbf{x}) = 0\}$ [S3, unofficial]. This allows handling sign-ambiguous geometry (thin sheets, open boundaries) while maintaining signed behavior for rendering [S3, unofficial].
